WEBVTT

00:00:09.631 --> 00:00:10.367
Hi there.

00:00:10.367 --> 00:00:14.207
Welcome to this video where we are
going to learn what private data is.

00:00:14.735 --> 00:00:18.687
Private data is information about you
that needs special protection.

00:00:19.167 --> 00:00:23.247
When you understand private data,
you can make safer choices online.

00:00:23.967 --> 00:00:27.135
The goal of this video is to help
you tell the difference between

00:00:27.135 --> 00:00:30.127
private data and public data.

00:00:30.127 --> 00:00:34.527
We will look at examples, explain
why attackers and companies want data,

00:00:34.607 --> 00:00:38.607
and practice a simple rule you can use
before you share anything online.

00:00:44.783 --> 00:00:47.583
When you use apps, games, or websites,

00:00:47.583 --> 00:00:51.023
you often share information,
sometimes without noticing

00:00:52.751 --> 00:00:56.063
some information is safe to share,
like your favorite color.

00:00:56.623 --> 00:00:58.783
Other information should stay private

00:00:58.783 --> 00:01:01.983
because it can identify you 
or help someone reach you.

00:01:03.231 --> 00:01:06.991
Private data is information about you
that should only be seen by you,

00:01:07.039 --> 00:01:10.031
your family, or trusted adults.

00:01:10.031 --> 00:01:14.031
Private data can help someone
figure out who you are or where you are.

00:01:14.831 --> 00:01:17.551
That is why private data needs extra care.

00:01:18.671 --> 00:01:21.231
Here are common examples of private data.

00:01:21.231 --> 00:01:25.279
Your full name, your home address, 
your phone number, your email address,

00:01:25.359 --> 00:01:28.351
your school name, and your passwords.

00:01:28.431 --> 00:01:31.039
Even one of these details can be important

00:01:31.039 --> 00:01:34.031
and several together can be very risky.

00:01:34.351 --> 00:01:38.239
Public data is information 
that is usually okay for most people to know.

00:01:38.879 --> 00:01:44.271
For example, your favorite sport,
your favorite food, or your favorite movie.

00:01:44.271 --> 00:01:46.271
Public data is still yours,

00:01:46.271 --> 00:01:49.839
but it normally does not help
someone find or identify you.

00:01:50.511 --> 00:01:55.279
Sometimes the same information can be safe
in one situation and risky in another.

00:01:55.951 --> 00:01:58.959
For example,
a pet's name might seem harmless,

00:01:59.071 --> 00:02:03.951
but it can be risky if someone uses it
to guess a password or security question.

00:02:04.959 --> 00:02:07.791
A big privacy problem is oversharing.

00:02:07.791 --> 00:02:12.111
Oversharing means sharing too much,
too often, or with too many people.

00:02:12.751 --> 00:02:16.111
Even small details can become risky
when they are combined,

00:02:16.191 --> 00:02:19.551
like your name, 
plus your school, plus your location.

00:02:24.943 --> 00:02:26.511
Companies may collect data

00:02:26.511 --> 00:02:30.431
to understand what you like
and to show you adverts or suggestions.

00:02:30.991 --> 00:02:34.783
They might track what you click,
what you watch, and what you search for.

00:02:35.823 --> 00:02:38.591
This is one reason privacy settings

00:02:38.591 --> 00:02:41.582
and careful sharing are important.

00:02:46.014 --> 00:02:49.198
Attackers may want 
private data for harmful reasons.

00:02:49.278 --> 00:02:51.470
They might try to create fake accounts,

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:54.670
pretend to be you, or trick
you later with messages.

00:02:55.198 --> 00:03:02.318
That is why passwords and contact
details should never be shared with strangers.

00:03:05.822 --> 00:03:07.950
Here is a simple rule you can use:

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:10.942
Stop and think before you share.

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:13.390
Ask yourself who will see this?

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:14.670
Why do they want it?

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:17.982
And what could go wrong
if the wrong person gets this information?

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:22.622
If you are unsure, the safest choice
is to ask a trusted adult.

00:03:23.262 --> 00:03:27.262
Trusted adults can help you decide
what is private and what is public.

00:03:27.982 --> 00:03:31.822
Asking for help is not a weakness,
it is a smart privacy skill.

00:03:35.118 --> 00:03:37.278
Let's recap.

00:03:37.278 --> 00:03:41.678
Private data is information that can
identify you or help someone reach you.

00:03:42.318 --> 00:03:45.438
Public data is usually safe,
like favorite things.

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:48.670
And context matters.

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:53.182
Think carefully before sharing,
especially in games, apps and messages.

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:55.550
Great work!

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:59.950
In this video, you learned what private
data is and why it needs protection.

00:04:00.718 --> 00:04:02.286
Thank you for watching.

00:04:02.286 --> 00:04:05.102
Do not forget to watch
other videos in our series.